      "text": "To find the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit past the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\dfrac{5}{14}\\), we first need to determine the decimal expansion of this fraction.\n\n1. **Find the decimal expansion of \\(\\dfrac{5}{14}\\):**\n\n   We can do this by performing the division:\n\n   \\[\n   \\dfrac{5}{14} = 0.3571428571428571 \\ldots\n   \\]\n\n   Here, the decimal expansion is \\(0.3571428571428571 \\ldots\\). This is a repeating decimal, with the repeating block \"357142\" repeating indefinitely.\n\n2. **Determine the length of the repeating block:**\n\n   The repeating block \"357142\" has 6 digits.\n\n3. **Find the position of the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit within the repeating cycle:**\n\n   To find the position of the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit, we need to calculate the remainder when 1314 is divided by 6 (the length of the repeating block).\n\n   \\[\n   1314 \\div 6 = 219 \\text{ remainder } 0\n   \\]\n\n   The remainder is 0, which means that 1314 is a multiple of the length of the repeating block (6). This implies that the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit corresponds to the last digit of the repeating block.\n\n4. **Identify the last digit of the repeating block:**\n\n   The repeating block is \"357142\". The last digit is '2'.\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n\n   The \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit past the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\dfrac{5}{14}\\) is \\(2\\).\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
